## XIOM in the Web Ecosystem

### Project Setup (wasm-pack style)

```sh
# Create WASM project
xiom init --wasm my-web-lib
cd my-web-lib

# Build for web
xiom build --target wasm32-unknown-unknown --wasm-bindgen

# Output:
# pkg/my_web_lib.js    (JS glue code)
# pkg/my_web_lib.wasm  (WebAssembly binary)
# pkg/my_web_lib.d.ts  (TypeScript declarations)
```

### Complex JS Interop

```xiom
use xiom::wasm_bindgen;

// Export to JavaScript
#[wasm_bindgen]
pub type ImageProcessor = {
  width: Int;
  height: Int;
  pixels: Vec[Byte];
}

#[wasm_bindgen]
impl ImageProcessor {
  pub fn new(width: Int, height: Int) -> ImageProcessor {
    return ImageProcessor{
      width: width,
      height: height,
      pixels: vec![0; width * height * 4],
    };
  }

  pub fn apply_grayscale(self: &mut ImageProcessor) {
    for i in (0..self.pixels.len()).step_by(4) {
      let r = self.pixels[i] as Float64;
      let g = self.pixels[i + 1] as Float64;
      let b = self.pixels[i + 2] as Float64;
      let gray = (0.299 * r + 0.587 * g + 0.114 * b) as Byte;
      self.pixels[i] = gray;
      self.pixels[i + 1] = gray;
      self.pixels[i + 2] = gray;
    }
  }

  // Return data to JS as Uint8Array
  pub fn get_pixels(self: &ImageProcessor) -> Vec[Byte] {
    return self.pixels;
  }
}
```

### JavaScript Usage

```javascript
import init, { ImageProcessor } from './pkg/my_web_lib.js';

async function processImage(imageData) {
  await init();

  const processor = ImageProcessor.new(imageData.width, imageData.height);

  // Pass pixel data from JS to WASM
  const pixels = new Uint8Array(imageData.data.buffer);
  processor.set_pixels(pixels);

  // Process in WASM (near-native speed)
  processor.apply_grayscale();

  // Get result back
  return processor.get_pixels();
}
```

### Canvas Rendering

```xiom
#[wasm_bindgen]
pub type Renderer = {
  ctx: CanvasRenderingContext2d,
}

#[wasm_bindgen]
impl Renderer {
  pub fn new(canvas_id: Str) -> Renderer {
    let ctx = get_canvas_context(canvas_id);
    return Renderer{ ctx: ctx };
  }

  pub fn draw_mandelbrot(self: &Renderer, center_x: Float64, center_y: Float64, zoom: Float64) {
    for py in 0..600 {
      for px in 0..800 {
        let x0 = (px as Float64 - 400.0) / zoom + center_x;
        let y0 = (py as Float64 - 300.0) / zoom + center_y;
        let iterations = mandelbrot_iter(x0, y0, 256);

        let color = if iterations == 256 {
          "black"
        } else {
          hsl_to_rgb(iterations * 360 / 256, 100, 50)
        };

        self.ctx.set_fill_style(color);
        self.ctx.fill_rect(px as Float64, py as Float64, 1.0, 1.0);
      }
    }
  }
}
```

### WASI (WebAssembly System Interface)

```xiom
// WASI provides filesystem, networking, and other OS features
use xiom::wasi;

fn main() -> Int {
  // Read from WASI filesystem
  let content = wasi::fs::read_to_str("/data/input.txt")?;

  // Process
  let processed = content.to_uppercase();

  // Write output
  wasi::fs::write("/data/output.txt", &processed)?;
  return 0;
}
```

```sh
# Run with WASI runtime
wasmtime --dir=data target/wasm32-wasi/debug/app.wasm
```

### Performance Considerations

| Operation | WASM Speed vs Native | Notes |
|-----------|---------------------|-------|
| Integer math | ~100% | Same as native |
| Float math | ~100% | Same as native |
| Memory access | ~95% | Slight overhead from bounds checks |
| Function calls | ~90% | Crossing WASM boundary has overhead |
| JS interop | ~50-80% | Depends on complexity |

Minimize JS ↔ WASM calls for best performance.

